<GuideLayout title="Storage Bin Size Guide" description="Interpret product dimensions correctly, including handles, lids, tapered sides, feet and stacking rims." canonicalPath="/guides/storage-bin-size-guide/" eyebrow="Sizing guide">
  <p>Storage-bin dimensions are not always presented in a consistent way. A retailer may show a capacity prominently, round the measurements or list dimensions without explaining whether the lid and handles are included. Fit planning requires the maximum external dimensions.</p>

  <h2>Width, height and depth need a fixed orientation</h2>
  <p>ShelfSketch assumes the item stands upright. Height is the vertical dimension. Width is the horizontal front dimension. Depth runs from front to back. If base rotation is allowed, width and depth may switch. Height remains vertical.</p>

  <h2>External dimensions versus internal capacity</h2>
  <p>Internal dimensions describe what can be stored. External dimensions describe what can fit into furniture. Always use external dimensions for the planner. Capacity in litres can help compare storage volume, but it cannot determine shelf fit.</p>

  <h2>Handles and grip lips</h2>
  <p>Built-in handles often project from the upper rim. Measure from the furthest left point to the furthest right point. If the handles face forward after rotation, they may instead affect depth.</p>

  <h2>Lids and locking clips</h2>
  <p>A lid may be wider than the body. Locking tabs can add several millimetres or centimetres. Use the dimensions of the closed container when that is how it will be stored.</p>

  <h2>Tapered walls</h2>
  <p>Many bins are narrower at the bottom so empty containers can nest. The widest top edge determines side-by-side fit. Do not use the base footprint unless the surrounding objects can genuinely occupy the sloped space.</p>

  <h2>Feet, wheels and stacking rims</h2>
  <p>Feet and wheels affect total height and sometimes width. Stacking rims can create a stable interlock but may require vertical lift before a container can be removed. Reserve clearance for the actual removal motion.</p>

  <h2>Soft and flexible containers</h2>
  <p>Fabric cubes and bags may compress when empty and bulge when full. Use a realistic filled measurement. ShelfSketch models them as rigid rectangles, so add a margin when the shape changes in use.</p>

  <h2>Rounded and irregular shapes</h2>
  <p>For a rounded basket, enter the smallest enclosing rectangular box: maximum width, maximum height and maximum depth. This is conservative but reduces the risk of an optimistic fit.</p>

  <h2>When product listings disagree</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Prefer the manufacturer’s technical drawing where available.</li>
    <li>Check whether units or dimension order differ.</li>
    <li>Look for wording such as “including handles” or “without lid”.</li>
    <li>Measure one physical unit before ordering multiples.</li>
    <li>Enter the larger plausible measurement when uncertainty remains.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="notice"><strong>Do not round down.</strong><br/>A few millimetres can determine whether an exact row fits. Preserve decimals shown by the manufacturer or your measuring tool.</div>
